title: Refreshing a record
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: reference
summary: POST …/refresh makes one record agree with its file now, with the watcher's semantics, so the watcher's own event for the same change writes nothing.
audience: dev


<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid/refresh">> `[?expected_version=<n>]`
stats the file at the record's path and writes, in one revision, every stat field
that differs from the stored one ([[File metadata fields]]). When the size or the
mtime moved, what was derived from the old content goes with it — the hashes and
the duplicate group — as on a watcher `Modify(Data)` ([[Event semantics]]). It
answers the metarecord, version included.

A state the record is already in writes nothing, so the version does not move:
the call is idempotent, and so is the watcher's refresh that follows it for the
same change. That is its use: a client that has just written a file itself (a
cross-repo sync copying, moving or re-moding one) refreshes the target record at
once, gets the version that describes the file, and the watcher's echo of that
same write then finds nothing to do. Without it the echo would arrive a quiet
period later and move the version the client had just recorded.

It differs from the watcher's refresh in one way: it writes the ''whole'' stat set
(`mfr_type`, `mfr_permissions`, …), not only the size and mtime — a record created
before its file exists has none of them yet.

It is a single-record write: `?expected_version=` fences it ([[Conditional writes]]).
A record with no path, or no entry on disk at it, is a `400`; nothing is written.
